  "title": "A CPA license may become a private-market passport",
  "question": "What did the SEC approve for proposal at its September 30, 2026 open meeting, how does the CPA route fit the wider private-market-access program, what was Peirce's artificial-intelligence point, and which legal and operating gates remain?",
  "bluf": "The SEC has asked for comment on treating an active U.S. CPA license as an accredited-investor credential; this is a live notice, not a final designation order.",
  "evidence_cutoff": "2026-09-30T20:55:02-04:00",
  "evidence_cutoff_note": "The complete 1:24:59 SEC YouTube recording caption export, meeting event page, agenda, Sunshine notice, press release, two proposed-rule pages and PDFs, combined fact sheet, four published Commissioner statements, five credential notices, ARK Order IC-36333, Notice IC-36308, controlling second amended application and ARK's EDGAR filing state through September 30 were checked. The transcript is a complete YouTube caption export, not an authenticated SEC verbatim transcript. The ARK order makes exemptive relief effective immediately but does not itself make either new class registration effective or prove venue admission, launch, trading, settlement or authoritative-record operation. Federal Register versions, exact comment deadlines and public comments were not yet available. NASBA's active-CPA count excludes Hawaii and New Mexico and is not a newly eligible investor count.",
  "use_note": "Use for the September 30 credential notices and CPA access proposal. Do not say CPAs qualify until a final Commission order issues. Keep notice, Federal Register publication, comment close, final designation, credential maintenance, issuer verification and any actual private-market purchase separate.",
